Relational SQL databases are like rigid, meticulously organized filing cabinets where every document must fit the exact same template.
NoSQL—which stands for 'Not Only SQL'—is a family of purpose-built storage engines that throw away that rigid table structure in exchange for flexible schemas,
massive horizontal scalability, and sub-millisecond performance on specific access patterns.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Instead of forcing all data into normalized rows and columns with expensive JOIN operations, 
NoSQL databases denormalize data to match how the application actually reads it. 
They fall into four primary architectural types:

Key-Value Stores (e.g., Redis, DynamoDB): The simplest model—a giant distributed hash map. 
You look up an opaque value using a unique key in $O(1)$ time. Ideal for caching, session management, and real-time leaderboards.

Document Stores (e.g., MongoDB, Couchbase): Store semi-structured, nested JSON or BSON documents where each record can have a completely different schema.
Ideal for product catalogs, user profiles, and content management.

Wide-Column Stores (e.g., Apache Cassandra, HBase): Organize data into dynamic columns grouped by a row key, distributed across massive clusters 
with no single point of failure. Ideal for extreme write-throughput workloads like IoT telemetry, time-series logs, and messaging history.

Graph Databases (e.g., Neo4j, Amazon Neptune): Store entities as Nodes and relationships as Edges, making multi-hop relationship traversals 
instantaneous instead of requiring brutal recursive SQL joins. Ideal for fraud rings, social networks, and recommendation engines.

Buisness Context :- 
Modern internet-scale applications—like a global e-commerce catalog where a laptop has 'RAM' and a t-shirt has 'Size'—break down in traditional 
relational databases due to constant schema migrations and vertical scaling ceilings. NoSQL lets product teams iterate rapidly without database
downtime while scaling horizontally across cheap commodity servers to serve millions of concurrent users.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers often pick NoSQL just to avoid writing SQL schemas, only to realize six months later that they need complex ad-hoc joins 
and multi-table ACID transactions—at which point they end up painfully rebuilding a relational database inside their application code.
As a data engineer, I follow Polyglot Persistence: I default to PostgreSQL for core transactional data, and only introduce a NoSQL store when a
specific access pattern demands it. Crucially, in NoSQL you must design your schema around your queries, not your entities—because if you don't know
your read patterns upfront, your 'web-scale' NoSQL database will quickly become slower than a single Postgres box.
